  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They held the lead for exactly one inning, then spent the rest chasing a deficit they couldn't close.",
    "what_this_means": "The Reds scored four runs in the second and the game never tightened after that. Mize exited after four innings with the damage already done. Four earned runs on five hits. And the bullpen that followed (Peralta, Morgan, Hart, Brito combining for 2.6 innings) couldn't reverse the math. This is what inconsistency looks like in real time: Ty France drove in two runs and the offense scraped together enough contact to stay within arm's reach, but ten strikeouts and a 1-for-7 showing in runners-in-scoring-position situations told the actual story. They had chances. They converted one. That wasn't enough against a team that had already built cushion. The sixth inning sat three runners on base with two outs. The critical miss. And the game had already tilted past recovery. This is a team that finds ways to stay close but rarely finds ways to finish what they start. They're ten games back now and every loss that looks winnable the next morning is the one that stings loudest.",
    "what_to_watch": "Max Fried brings a 2.80 ERA into tonight against Walker Buehler, who's been sharp over his last four starts at 2.74. This is a matchup that rewards early execution. Get runners on, turn it into runs before the pitcher finds rhythm.",
